            <ArticleTitle>Body mass index influence interferon-beta treatment response in multiple sclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>92-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jneuroim.2015.09.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0165-5728(15)30043-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is a possible risk factor of multiple sclerosis (MS), but the association between obesity and MS disease activity has not been explored. In a cohort of 86 MS patients, 80% of overweight or obese patients (BMI≥25kg/m(2)) had MRI activity compared to 48% of the normal-weight patients (BMI&lt;25kg/m(2)) (p=0.001) during interferon-beta treatment. NEDA-status (no evidence of disease activity) was defined as a composite that consisted of absence of any relapses, sustained disability-progression and MRI-activity. Among normal-weight patients 26% obtained NEDA-status compared to only 13% of patients with BMI &gt;25 (p=0.05). This may indicate that BMI affects interferon-beta treatment response.</AbstractText>
